-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Money</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What it costs to live in Myrtle Beach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Housing is where you gain. A typical home here costs about $342,000. The same measure is about $737,000 in the New York area, $742,000 in Boston and $580,000 in Washington. Rent is about 17 percent below the national average.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Everything else is closer to normal. Food, power and everyday items sit near the national average, so do not plan on the grocery bill falling. Pay is lower here too, which matters if you are still working.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Two costs are new. South Carolina taxes your car every year, which most northern states do not, and a second home is taxed at about three times the rate of a main home. Both are covered on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">cost of living page</a>, which compares your city with this one directly.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Money</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What it costs to live in Myrtle Beach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Housing is where you gain. A typical home here costs about $342,000. The same measure is about $737,000 in the New York area, $742,000 in Boston and $580,000 in Washington. Rent is about 17 percent below the national average.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Everything else is closer to normal. Food, power and everyday items sit near the national average, so do not plan on the grocery bill falling. <a href="/buyers/relocating/jobs/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pay is lower here too</a>, which matters if you are still working.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Two costs are new. South Carolina taxes your car every year, which most northern states do not, and a second home is taxed at about three times the rate of a main home. Both are covered on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">cost of living page</a>, which compares your city with this one directly.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Weather</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What the weather is like</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Winters are mild</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">January averages a high near 56 and a low in the mid 30s. Snow is rare: about an inch a year on average, and most years get none. Two recent winters each produced a 5 inch storm, and the record is 14 inches from a Christmas storm in 1989. When it does snow, roads and schools close for a day or two, because there is no reason to own plows here.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Summers are hot and humid</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">July averages a high near 87 and a low near 72. The area gets about 22 days a year at or above 90 degrees and almost none at 100, so the raw temperature is not extreme. The humidity is what people feel. Most newcomers say they adjust within a season or two.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">It is brighter than most places people leave</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The nearest long term weather station records about 62 percent of possible sunshine and 111 clear days a year. Pittsburgh averages about 42 percent, Buffalo about 47 percent, and Cleveland about 66 clear days. People from those cities notice it in the first month. The month by month numbers, including ocean water temperatures, are on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/weather/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">weather page</a>.</p></div></section>
 
