 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Step one</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Check the exact address, not the neighborhood</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Horry County</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The district runs an <a href="https://hcsd1.maps.arcgis.com/apps/instant/lookup/index.html?appid=72f93a5329c54f54840673fe6430ab43" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Attendance Zone Lookup</a> that takes a street address and returns the assigned schools. The district&#39;s own instruction is to use it if you are new to the area and unsure, and to call the technology helpdesk at 843-488-6825 if questions remain. The tool is address-based, so a house under construction may not return a result until the lot has a posted address.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The district is organized into nine attendance areas: Aynor, Carolina Forest, Conway, Green Sea Floyds, Loris, Myrtle Beach, North Myrtle Beach, Socastee, and St. James. It publishes a feeder chart showing which elementary schools lead to which middle and high schools, on its <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/about-hcs/our-schools" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Our Schools</a> page.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Georgetown County</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">There is no public address lookup. Call the district office at 843-436-7000, or the school serving the area, and ask for the zoned school for that street address. Ask for it by email so you have it in writing. The district groups its schools into four areas, and the one covering Pawleys Island, Litchfield, and the Waccamaw Neck is the Waccamaw group.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One practical note for both districts: buses. Horry County does not transport students across attendance boundaries, and Georgetown County provides bus service to students living more than a mile and a half from their zoned school. If a child is close to a boundary, ask the district&#39;s transportation office what that means for your street before you commit.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">This comes up in real transactions. One family moved here because their oldest child was starting at Coastal Carolina, and their younger child still needed a school. They knew which zone they wanted, and the houses they liked sat near its edge, some inside it and some not. We checked each address against the district lookup, told them which was which, and gave no opinion on any school. They picked with the full picture, and the younger child enrolled where the family intended. That is the service: not an opinion, a confirmed address.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Looking at a specific house? We will pull the zone with you and get it confirmed.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask about an address</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">What to check</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Zones change, and this area is building schools</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County is one of the faster-growing counties in the country and the district builds to keep up. When two elementary schools opened in Carolina Forest, the board redrew the lines for three existing elementary schools and published the list of affected subdivisions by name. That process took roughly four months from first draft to final vote, and the new lines took effect the following August. Another replacement school is in the district&#39;s capital plan.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two things follow. First, the school names in a home&#39;s advertisement are not a district record. Agents and websites copy them forward for years without checking. Second, a zone you confirm in March can change for the school year after next. If a specific school assignment is the reason you are buying a particular house, confirm it with the district in writing, and understand you are confirming today&#39;s line.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Capacity affects transfers, not enrollment</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Each year Horry County publishes a list of schools at or above 95 percent of designed capacity, which the district calls Category 1 or Red Zone. A student zoned for one of those schools always enrolls there. The designation closes that school to elective transfers from outside its zone, with no exceptions for siblings or hardship. If your plan depends on transferring in, check the current list first. The list is republished every spring on the district&#39;s <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/departments-services/registration/student-transfer-information" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">student transfer</a> page.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">What to check</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Zones change, and this area is building schools</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County is <a href="/buyers/relocating/why-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">one of the faster-growing counties in the country</a> and the district builds to keep up. When two elementary schools opened in Carolina Forest, the board redrew the lines for three existing elementary schools and published the list of affected subdivisions by name. That process took roughly four months from first draft to final vote, and the new lines took effect the following August. Another replacement school is in the district&#39;s capital plan.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two things follow. First, the school names in a home&#39;s advertisement are not a district record. Agents and websites copy them forward for years without checking. Second, a zone you confirm in March can change for the school year after next. If a specific school assignment is the reason you are buying a particular house, confirm it with the district in writing, and understand you are confirming today&#39;s line.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Capacity affects transfers, not enrollment</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Each year Horry County publishes a list of schools at or above 95 percent of designed capacity, which the district calls Category 1 or Red Zone. A student zoned for one of those schools always enrolls there. The designation closes that school to elective transfers from outside its zone, with no exceptions for siblings or hardship. If your plan depends on transferring in, check the current list first. The list is republished every spring on the district&#39;s <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/departments-services/registration/student-transfer-information" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">student transfer</a> page.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The official data</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Read the state&#39;s report card yourself</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina publishes an annual report card for every public school and district at <a href="https://screportcards.com/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">screportcards.com</a>. Each school&#39;s page carries an overall rating on a five-step scale, along with the underlying measures, and the site lets you compare schools and download the raw data files. It is a state publication, its methodology is public, and it is the source both districts point to.</p><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);max-width:720px;margin:1.2rem 0;padding:.4rem 0">
 <div style="display:flex;gap:.8rem;padding:.7rem 0;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--brass);flex:none;line-height:1.3">1</span><span>Type the school&#39;s name in the Search for Your School box, or choose View Report Cards and browse by district.</span></div>
