-</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What waterfront means on the Grand Strand</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Waterfront in a Myrtle Beach listing can mean five different things: oceanfront, Intracoastal Waterway frontage, tidal channel or creek frontage, riverfront, or a lake and pond lot. They price differently, insure differently, and boat differently. The first question on any waterfront listing is which one it is.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">The second question is what you want the water for. Looking at it, fishing it, and running a boat to the ocean are three different purchases, and plenty of buyers pay for the third and get the first.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The five kinds</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The five kinds of waterfront, and where they are</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:720px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Oceanfront.</strong> Mostly condo buildings. True oceanfront houses are scarce and cluster toward Cherry Grove in the north and the Pawleys Island and Litchfield stretch in the south.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Intracoastal Waterway.</strong> The main boating corridor: Socastee, the Carolina Forest edge, and up through Little River. Protected water, dock potential, and long no-wake stretches.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Tidal channels and creeks.</strong> Cherry Grove&#39;s channel grid and the Murrells Inlet creeks. Depth changes with the tide, so the low-tide number is the one that matters.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Riverfront.</strong> The Waccamaw and the Conway riverwalk side. Freshwater, quieter, further from the inlets.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Lakes and ponds.</strong> Water to look at, not to boat. Priced accordingly, and there is nothing wrong with that if the view is what you want.</span></li></ul><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Want us to look at it for you?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Set up a waterfront tour</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Docks</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Docks and dock permits, the part that decides value</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the Intracoastal and the tidal creeks, a private dock usually needs a state permit through DHEC&#39;s coastal management office, plus whatever your HOA allows. Not every lot qualifies, and some neighborhoods use shared day docks instead of private ones.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">Permitting commonly takes a few months, often around four, before construction starts, and most permitted residential docks here cost somewhere between 15,000 and 45,000 dollars to build. That wait and that cost are why a home with an existing permitted dock carries a premium. They are also why we verify the permit status and transferability of every dock during due diligence instead of taking the advertisement&#39;s word for it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Flood and insurance</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Flood zones and insurance, the honest version</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of the high-risk flood map here is zones AE and VE. VE is the coastal high-hazard zone where wave action is part of the calculation, and it is the most expensive water to insure.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Averages mislead on this. Across Horry County, flood policies average a few hundred dollars a year, but true waterfront in AE or VE commonly runs between one and three thousand dollars a year, and sometimes more. Wind and hail coverage is a separate question on top.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">Get an exact flood quote on the specific address during due diligence. We can recommend local insurance agents who write flood coverage here, just ask. How the policies stack is covered in our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal insurance guide</a>, and you can <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">check whether the house has flooded before</a>.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Want us to look at it for you?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Set up a waterfront tour</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Boat access</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Six questions to ask before you buy for the boat</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:720px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">How deep is the water at low tide?</strong> At the dock, not the channel. This is the number that decides what boat you can keep.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">What bridges sit between you and open water?</strong> Fixed bridge clearance against your boat&#39;s height, at high tide.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">How long is the no-wake ride to the inlet?</strong> Minutes on the map are not minutes on the water.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Is the dock permit current and transferable?</strong> An unpermitted dock is a liability, not a feature.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Who owns and maintains the bulkhead?</strong> Bulkhead repairs are expensive, and responsibility varies by lot and HOA.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">What does the HOA allow?</strong> Boat lifts, dock size, overnight tie-ups, and rentals all vary by neighborhood.</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">The ocean runs through the inlets, most directly Little River Inlet in the north and Murrells Inlet in the south. The route, not the distance, decides how long the ride takes.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Where boaters buy</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Where boaters buy on the Grand Strand</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;"><a href="/submarkets/little-river/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Little River</a> has the most marinas and the quickest big-water access. <a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Cherry Grove in North Myrtle Beach</a> runs on its tidal channel grid with ocean access through the inlet. The Intracoastal corridor through Socastee and <a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Carolina Forest</a> offers protected water with long no-wake stretches. <a href="/submarkets/murrells-inlet/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Murrells Inlet</a> pairs creek frontage with the MarshWalk and the southern inlet. The <a href="/submarkets/pawleys-island/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pawleys Island</a> and Litchfield marsh side in Georgetown County is further from the inlets and quieter.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;padding:3rem 1.5rem"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.9rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the boat and the budget.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);max-width:560px;margin:0 auto 1.6rem;line-height:1.7">We will tell you which water works, and check the dock, the depth, and the flood zone before you fall for the view.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#lead-form">Set up a waterfront tour</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135" style="color:var(--ivory);border-color:rgba(244,239,232,.4)">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Waterfront FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What counts as waterfront in Myrtle Beach listings?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Five different things: oceanfront, Intracoastal Waterway frontage, tidal channel or creek frontage, riverfront, and lake or pond lots. They price differently, insure differently, and boat differently, so always ask which one a listing means.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I build a private dock?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">On the Intracoastal and tidal creeks, usually yes, with a state permit through DHEC's coastal management office plus whatever your HOA allows. Not every lot qualifies, and some neighborhoods use shared day docks instead of private ones.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How long does a dock permit take?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Commonly a few months, often around four, before construction can start. A home that already has a permitted dock skips that wait, which is part of why existing docks add real value.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How much does a dock cost to build?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Most permitted residential docks on the coast land somewhere between 15,000 and 45,000 dollars built, depending on length, height, and finishes.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How much is flood insurance on the water?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Away from the water, Horry County flood policies average a few hundred dollars a year. True waterfront in the higher-risk AE and VE zones often runs between one and three thousand dollars a year, sometimes more. Get an exact quote on the specific address during due diligence, and we can recommend local agents who write flood coverage here.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I reach the ocean by boat from the Intracoastal?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes, through the inlets, most directly Little River Inlet in the north and Murrells Inlet in the south. Check fixed bridge clearances and no-wake stretches on your route, because they decide how long the ride takes.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What should I check before buying a waterfront home?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Water depth at low tide, dock permit status and transferability, bulkhead condition and ownership, the flood zone and real insurance quotes, HOA water rules, and the route to open water. We check all of it as part of the offer work.</p></div></div></section>
