0px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">How deep is the water at low tide?</strong> At the dock, not the channel. This is the number that decides what boat you can keep.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">What bridges sit between you and open water?</strong> Fixed bridge clearance against your boat&#39;s height, at high tide.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">How long is the no-wake ride to the inlet?</strong> Minutes on the map are not minutes on the water.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Is the dock permit current and transferable?</strong> An unpermitted dock is a liability, not a feature.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Who owns and maintains the bulkhead?</strong> Bulkhead repairs are expensive, and responsibility varies by lot and HOA.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">What does the HOA allow?</strong> Boat lifts, dock size, overnight tie-ups, and rentals all vary by neighborhood.</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">The ocean runs through the inlets, most directly Little River Inlet in the north and Murrells Inlet in the south. The route, not the distance, decides how long the ride takes.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Where boaters buy</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Where boaters buy on the Grand Strand</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;"><a href="/submarkets/little-river/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Little River</a> has the most marinas and the quickest big-water access. <a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Cherry Grove in North Myrtle Beach</a> runs on its tidal channel grid with ocean access through the inlet. The Intracoastal corridor through Socastee and <a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Carolina Forest</a> offers protected water with long no-wake stretches. <a href="/submarkets/murrells-inlet/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Murrells Inlet</a> pairs creek frontage with the MarshWalk and the southern inlet. The <a href="/submarkets/pawleys-island/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pawleys Island</a> and Litchfield marsh side in Georgetown County is further from the inlets and quieter.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;padding:3rem 1.5rem"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.9rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the boat and the budget.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);max-width:560px;margin:0 auto 1.6rem;line-height:1.7">We will tell you which water works, and check the dock, the depth, and the flood zone before you fall for the view.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#lead-form">Set up a waterfront tour</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135" style="color:var(--ivory);border-color:rgba(244,239,232,.4)">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Waterfront FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What counts as waterfront in Myrtle Beach listings?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Five different things: oceanfront, Intracoastal Waterway frontage, tidal channel or creek frontage, riverfront, and lake or pond lots. They price differently, insure differently, and boat differently, so always ask which one a listing means.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I build a private dock?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">On the Intracoastal and tidal creeks, usually yes, with a state permit through DHEC's coastal management office plus whatever your HOA allows. Not every lot qualifies, and some neighborhoods use shared day docks instead of private ones.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How long does a dock permit take?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Commonly a few months, often around four, before construction can start. A home that already has a permitted dock skips that wait, which is part of why existing docks add real value.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How much does a dock cost to build?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Most permitted residential docks on the coast land somewhere between 15,000 and 45,000 dollars built, depending on length, height, and finishes.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How much is flood insurance on the water?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Away from the water, Horry County flood policies average a few hundred dollars a year. True waterfront in the higher-risk AE and VE zones often runs between one and three thousand dollars a year, sometimes more. Get an exact quote on the specific address during due diligence, and we can recommend local agents who write flood coverage here.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I reach the ocean by boat from the Intracoastal?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes, through the inlets, most directly Little River Inlet in the north and Murrells Inlet in the south. Check fixed bridge clearances and no-wake stretches on your route, because they decide how long the ride takes.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What should I check before buying a waterfront home?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Water depth at low tide, dock permit status and transferability, bulkhead condition and ownership, the flood zone and real insurance quotes, HOA water rules, and the route to open water. We check all of it as part of the offer work.</p></div></div></section>
 </div>
 </main>
