line}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(3){view-timeline:--c3ph-p3 inline}.c3ph-dots i{animation:c3ph-dot linear both;animation-range:cover}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(1){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p1}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(2){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p2}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(3){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p3}@keyframes c3ph-dot{0%,36%{background:transparent}42%,60%{background:var(--navy)}66%,100%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:760px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Five things in the papers should stop you, in a house, a townhouse or a condo. A rule that blocks your plan comes first, because a lower price does not fix it. The other four cost you money after you buy.</p><ul class="hd-flags" aria-label="Red flags in HOA documents, where each shows up"><li class="hot"><span class="k">In the declaration and the rules</span><b>A rule that blocks your plan</b>A limit on renting, pets, parking, boats or golf carts that rules out how you will use the home. A condo's founding papers must state any limit on renting a unit.<span class="w">More on <a href="/hoa/rental-restrictions/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental rules</a></span></li><li><span class="k">In the board's minutes</span><b>A special assessment on the way</b>A special assessment is a one-time bill on top of the dues, for a cost the budget and the savings do not cover. South Carolina's condo and HOA laws set no cap on one. We read the minutes for one being planned.<span class="w">What a <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessment</a> can cost</span></li><li><span class="k">In the budget and the reserve study</span><b>Thin savings for big repairs</b>South Carolina does not require an HOA or a condo to save for big repairs. A reserve study shows what the savings should hold today. One <a href="https://www.reservestudy.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/2026-04-29-100000-Industry-Insights-Report-FINAL.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">national study firm</a> calls under 30 percent of that weak, with a high chance of special assessments, and 70 percent or more strong.<span class="w">How we read <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA reserves</a></span></li><li><span class="k">In the rules and the declaration</span><b>Strict fine and collection rules</b>In the past 12 months, Horry County associations filed 60 fine policies and 43 collection policies for unpaid dues. In one <a href="https://cases.justia.com/south-carolina/supreme-court/2019-27934.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina case</a>, the court judgment came to more than 11 times the unpaid dues, after interest, court costs and lawyer's fees.<span class="w">How an <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA lien</a> works</span></li><li><span class="k">In the association's statement</span><b>Dues the seller still owes</b>Unpaid dues are usually a lien paid from the seller's money at closing. If the sale leaves nothing to pay them, the association may go after the new owner, or waive them. In a condo, the buyer owes no more than the association's statement says.<span class="w">What the <a href="/hoa/estoppel-and-transfer-fees/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">closing statement</a> shows</span></li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">In a condo: the lender's building review</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the home is a condo, the lender also reviews the whole building. The <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.2-02/full-review-process" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tests</a> come from Fannie Mae, a company that buys home loans from lenders. A house in an HOA <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-01/general-information-project-standards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">skips this review</a>, apart from some basic rules.</p><div class="hd-condorow"><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 759px) 100vw, (max-width: 1179px) 720px, 420px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Older two-story houses on lawns along the beach at left, and two tall condo towers on the beach at right, with streets of houses under oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses and oceanfront towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach. For a condo in a tower, the lender reviews the whole building. A house in an HOA skips that review.</figcaption></figure></div><ul class="hd-tests" aria-label="The lender's tests for a condo building"><li><b>Savings</b>In Fannie Mae's full review of a condo building, the budget must put at least 10 percent of dues income into savings. Freddie Mac, which also buys home loans, raises its test to 15 percent for loan applications from January 4, 2027.</li><li><b>Owners behind on dues</b>No more than 15 percent of units can be 60 days or more behind. In a 100-unit building, that is 15 units.</li><li><b>Repairs put off</b>A building that needs critical repairs, or has major upkeep put off, does not pass.</li><li><b>Lawsuits</b>A suit naming the association can block a normal loan. A suit against the builder about safety, structure, use or whether units can be lived in can too.</li><li><b>Who owns the space</b>Shops and offices can fill no more than 35 percent of the building. In a building of 21 or more units, one owner can hold no more than 20 percent of them.</li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a building fails, a loan on a unit there cannot be sold to Fannie Mae. Buyers then need a different loan, often at a higher cost. Buildings with a rental program here usually fail, because so many owners are investors.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you pay cash, no lender reviews the building for you. When you sell, a buyer who needs a loan may face the same review.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How our agents read the rules</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents read the rules against how you plan to live: a boat, a golf cart, a rental, a pool. For a buyer who plans to rent, we leave out communities that ban renting before the search starts. Then we read the HOA papers for any rule that would undo the plan.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Will a lender approve the building?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the building's name. We read its budget and minutes against the lender's tests, both savings tests included, and tell you which ones it fails.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us test the building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="the-documents"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The documents">Which HOA documents should you get before buying?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ten papers, from four places: the county, the association, the seller and your lender. Most buyers here need them. In our listing search, nearly every condo, 9 of 10 townhouses and about 7 of 10 houses for sale list an association fee.</p><figure class="hd-fig hd-docfig"><p class="cap" id="hd-docs-cap">The HOA documents to get, and what each one tells you</p><table class="hd-docs" aria-labelledby="hd-docs-cap"><thead><tr><th scope="col">The document</th><th scope="col">Where it comes from</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Declaration<small>Also called the CC&amp;Rs, short for covenants, conditions and restrictions. A condo's is called the master deed.</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Recorded at the county</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What you own, what is shared, your share of the costs and any limits on renting</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bylaws</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Recorded at the county</td><td data-label="What it tells you">How the board is elected, how votes work and how the rules get changed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Rules and regulations</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association; also filed at the county</td><td data-label="What it tells you">The day-to-day rules: pets, parking, the pool, trash, what you can build</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Budget, balance sheet and income statement</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What comes in, what goes out, and <a href="/hoa/dues-increases/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">whether the dues went up this year</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Reserve study<small>A list of the big shared parts, such as the roof and the paving</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">When each part needs replacing, what it will cost, and whether the savings cover it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meeting minutes, 12 to 24 months</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the board plans next: repairs, a special assessment, rule changes, a lawsuit</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Master insurance certificate</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the building's policy covers and what you insure yourself. See <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">master and HO-6 insurance</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Statement of amounts due<small>Also called an estoppel letter</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association, before closing</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the seller still owes the association</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Condo questionnaire<small>A form the lender sends the association</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Your lender</td><td data-label="What it tells you">Owners behind on dues, lawsuits, commercial space: the answers the building review needs</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Seller's disclosure statement, with its HOA addendum</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The seller, before the contract</td><td data-label="What it tells you">The seller's answers to 11 yes-or-no questions about the association</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">The recorded papers for one address</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the address. We pull the declaration and the bylaws from the county and mark the rules on renting, pets and parking.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us pull the declaration</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="sc-law"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What SC law requires">Does an HOA have to give a buyer its documents in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. No South Carolina law makes an association give a buyer a resale packet, gives it a deadline, or caps its fee. The state's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HOA law</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">condo law</a> and <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nonprofit law</a> have no such rule.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's duty is a form. Unless the sale is exempt, the seller fills in the state's <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">disclosure form</a> before the contract is signed. If the home is in an association, that includes a one-page HOA addendum.</p><div class="hd-two"><div><p class="k">The seller's form asks 11 yes-or-no questions</p><ul><li>Dues and other charges</li><li>Limits on reselling or renting</li><li>Special assessments</li><li>Limits on guests</li><li>Limits on pets</li><li>Assigned parking, lockers or garages</li><li>Keys, fobs or codes for shared areas</li><li>Other memberships that come with the home</li><li>Known problems in shared areas</li><li>Whether the home is under the state's coastal zone law</li><li>A transfer fee</li></ul></div><div class="only"><p class="k">Only the papers show</p><ul><li>The exact words of each rule</li><li>How much the association has saved</li><li>What the board plans next</li><li>What the seller still owes</li><li>Whether a lender will approve a condo building</li></ul></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller may answer any question "No Representation", which means no answer at all. The form itself tells buyers to read the documents before signing a contract. If the seller never gives the form, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the law says</a> the contract still stands and the closing goes ahead.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some sales skip the form. Examples are the first sale of a home no one has lived in, a foreclosure sale and an auction. A sale is also exempt when both sides agree in writing.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What other states give a buyer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buyers who move here from Virginia, Maryland or Florida often expect the papers to arrive by law, with days to cancel. Maryland and Florida are among the states that send the most movers to Horry County.</p><ul class="hd-states" aria-label="What a buyer of a home in an association gets by law in four other states"><li><b>Virginia</b><span class="k">What the buyer gets by law</span>The association's resale certificate. Unless the sale is exempt, the seller must get it for the buyer.<span class="k">The way out</span>If the contract names no period, 3 days after the buyer gets it. Any time before settlement if it never comes.</li><li><b>Maryland</b><span class="k">What the buyer gets by law</span>The HOA disclosures, within 20 calendar days of the contract. The law caps the association's fee for them.<span class="k">The way out</span>5 calendar days to cancel if they come late.</li><li><b>Florida, in an HOA</b><span class="k">What the buyer gets by law</span>A disclosure summary before the buyer signs.<span class="k">The way out</span>If it came late: void the contract within 3 days of getting it or before closing, whichever comes first.</li><li><b>North Carolina, in a condo</b><span class="k">What the buyer gets by law</span>In most condo resales, a statement of the monthly dues and other fees, before closing.<span class="k">The way out</span>None. This law has no days to cancel.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A bill now in a House committee, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess126_2025-2026/bills/5204.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">H. 5204</a>, would require an HOA seller packet on a website at no charge. It would also require the seller certification and governing documents within 15 days of the seller's request. It has not moved out of committee and is not law.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="public-record"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Public record">Are HOA documents public record in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, the recorded ones. An association's declaration, its bylaws, their amendments and its rules must be filed with the county's <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/register-of-deeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Register of Deeds</a> to be enforced. The county's <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">index</a> is online, but the pages are not, so we pull them at the county and send you the copies.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The papers the county does not keep</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The budget, the minutes, the reserve study and the insurance certificate come from the association or its manager. The seller, who still owns the home, can ask for them, and we ask the seller and the manager as soon as you are under contract.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Need the budget and the minutes?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We get them from the seller and the manager, then mark what the savings, the dues and the board's plans mean for you.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask us to get the budget and minutes</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="rules-change"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Rules change">How often do HOA rules change in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often. In the 12 months to October 2026, about 700 association papers were recorded in Horry County. More than 300 associations filed them, and about 400 were rules and policies.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before 2018, almost no rules were on file. Associations that already existed had until January 10, 2019, to record theirs under a 2018 law, and 554 papers were filed that January alone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rules you read under contract can change after you buy. A new rule takes effect when the board adopts it. It must reach the county by January 10 of the next year, so until then only the association's copy shows it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="when-they-arrive"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="When they arrive">When do you get HOA documents, and what if you find a problem?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's form comes before the contract. Everything else arrives during your review time, and only if someone asks for it. We ask the day you sign.</p><ol class="hd-steps" aria-label="When the HOA papers arrive, in order"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before you offer</span><b>The seller's form, and the recorded papers</b>The seller's HOA answers come with the disclosure statement. We pull the recorded declaration from the county.</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><span class="t">When you sign</span><b>Your review time starts</b>South Carolina law has no set number of days for the HOA papers. The review time in your contract, often called the due diligence period, is your clock.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><span class="t">During the review</span><b>The papers the association keeps</b>We ask for the rules, budget, minutes, reserve study and insurance certificate at once. Your lender sends the condo questionnaire.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before closing</span><b>What the seller still owes</b>The association's statement shows any unpaid dues. We check for them before closing.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><span class="t">After you buy</span><b>The rules can change</b>The board can adopt new rules. The minutes you read under contract show what it plans next.</span></li></ol><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If the papers show a problem</h3><ul class="hd-flags hd-fix" aria-label="What to do if the papers show a problem"><li><span class="k">A line you cannot read</span><b>The association explains it</b>We ask the manager in writing what the line in the budget or minutes means, and how much any coming bill will be.</li><li><span class="k">A debt or a coming bill</span><b>The seller pays or fixes it</b>We ask the seller to pay what is owed, or to take a coming bill off the price.</li><li class="hot"><span class="k">A rule that blocks your plan</span><b>You walk away</b>No price cut fixes it. We tell you while you can still leave on your contract's terms.</li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Under contract already?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the address and the last day of your review time. We get the papers, read them first and call you with what we found.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us your review deadline</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="too-late"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Too late">What happens to buyers who read HOA documents too late?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many owners who complain to the state got the papers too late. Of Horry County owners who complained about an HOA from 2022 through 2025, about two in three got the papers after they bought.</p><figure class="hd-fig"><p class="cap">Horry County owners who complained to the state about an HOA</p><p class="hd-q">When did they get the HOA papers?</p><div class="hd-stack"><svg role="img" aria-label="Of 359 who answered, 124 got the papers before buying and 235 after buying" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none"><title>Of 359 who answered, 124 got the papers before buying and 235 after buying</title><rect x="0.00" y="0" width="34.54" height="10" fill="#5c6b78"/><rect x="34.54" y="0" width="65.46" height="10" fill="#a8482f"/></svg><span aria-hidden="true" style="left:0.00%;width:34.54%">35%</span><span aria-hidden="true" style="left:34.54%;width:65.46%">65%</span></div><ul class="hd-key"><li><i class="a" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>124</b> before they bought</span></li><li><i class="c" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>235</b> after they bought</span></li></ul><p class="hd-q">When were they told the home was in an HOA?</p><div class="hd-stack"><svg role="img" aria-label="Of 372 who answered, 198 were told before closing, 119 at closing and 55 after closing" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none"><title>Of 372 who answered, 198 were told before closing, 119 at closing and 55 after closing</title><rect x="0.00" y="0" width="53.23" height="10" fill="#5c6b78"/><rect x="53.23" y="0" width="31.99" height="10" fill="#8a5226"/><rect x="85.22" y="0" width="14.78" height="10" fill="#a8482f"/></svg><span aria-hidden="true" style="left:0.00%;width:53.23%">53%</span><span aria-hidden="true" style="left:53.23%;width:31.99%">32%</span><span aria-hidden="true" style="left:85.22%;width:14.78%">15%</span></div><ul class="hd-key"><li><i class="a" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>198</b> before closing</span></li><li><i class="b" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>119</b> at closing</span></li><li><i class="c" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>55</b> after closing</span></li></ul><figcaption>Complaints to SC Consumer Affairs from 2022 through 2025. Each bar counts only the owners who answered that question. These are owners who complained, not all owners.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Of the 413 who answered, 55 said they had been denied access to the governing documents, and 49 said they had not received a copy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More of the complaints in the <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">state's 2026 HOA report</a> came from Horry County than from any other county: 31 percent of 452.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents get the papers in front of you while you can still walk away, and read them for you first.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="hoa-documents-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">HOA documents FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are HOA documents public record in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The recorded ones are: the declaration, the bylaws, their amendments and the rules. In Horry County the index is online; we pull the pages at the county for you. The rest come from the association.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I back out of buying a house because of the HOA documents?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not under a South Carolina law. Your contract's review time is when to act. In Virginia, Maryland and Florida, a buyer has a set number of days by law.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long does it take to get HOA documents?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina law has no deadline for them, so it depends on the association. That is why we ask the manager for the papers and a date as soon as you are under contract.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who pays for HOA documents in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No South Carolina law caps the association's fee for them or names an amount. We find out what the manager charges before the papers are ordered, so the cost is known up front.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the seller have to tell me there is an HOA?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, unless the sale is exempt, such as the first sale of a new home. The seller's disclosure form asks, and a yes brings the one-page HOA addendum. A missing form does not undo the contract.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-30</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 33-31</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure form</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/t3wpam13/customer-service-request-form.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry deeds office</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-01/general-information-project-standards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-01</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.2-02/full-review-process" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.2-02</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/bulletin/2026-C" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac 2026-C</a>, <a href="https://cases.justia.com/south-carolina/supreme-court/2019-27934.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Winrose v. Hale</a>, <a href="https://www.reservestudy.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/2026-04-29-100000-Industry-Insights-Report-FINAL.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Association Reserves 2026</a>, <a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter23.1/section55.1-2312/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Virginia 55.1-2312</a>, <a href="https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Laws/StatuteText?article=grp&section=11B-106" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maryland 11B-106</a>, <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2026/720.401" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Florida 720.401</a>, <a href="https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_47C/GS_47C-4-109.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NC 47C-4-109</a>, <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDCA HOA reports</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess126_2025-2026/bills/5204.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC H. 5204</a>. Our count of the county's deed index and the state's complaint files; listings in aggregate.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, and nothing on this page is legal advice. For advice on your own contract, talk to a South Carolina attorney.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Houses and oceanfront towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 crops.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":782,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Houses and oceanfront towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach","caption":"Houses and oceanfront towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach. For a condo in a tower, the lender reviews the whole building. A house in an HOA skips that review.","description":"Older two-story houses on lawns along the beach at left, and two tall condo towers on the beach at right, with streets of houses under oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"The oceanfront, Myrtle Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Get the HOA papers read before you are committed.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Send the community name. We get the papers, read them and tell you what we found.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us get and read the papers</a></div></section></main>
