-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What do HOA fees cover in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends entirely on the association. On a Grand Strand condo, dues often carry the building's master insurance, exterior and roof maintenance, elevators, pools, trash, pest control, and frequently water, sewer and cable. On a single-family HOA they may cover only common areas and a pool. The only way to know is to read that association's budget."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are low HOA fees a good sign?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by themselves. Low dues usually mean the association provides fewer services and covers fewer utilities. They can also mean the association is underfunding its reserves, which shows up later as a special assessment. Compare what the dues include before you compare the dues."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are Horry County HOA complaints so high?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Horry County files more HOA complaints than any other county in South Carolina. In the state's 2026 report it accounted for 31 percent of all complaints and led the state per capita at 0.38 per 1,000 residents. Georgetown County was second per capita. Dense condo and planned-community development in a fast-growing coastal market is the likely driver."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I refuse to join an HOA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. If the property is inside an association, membership and assessments run with the land. You agree to them when you buy. That is why reading the governing documents before you make an offer matters so much."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there a state agency that oversees South Carolina HOAs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No one, in the sense most owners expect. The South Carolina Department of Consumer Affairs collects HOA complaints because the law requires it, and publishes an annual report. But it does not administer or enforce the Homeowners Association Act, and its complaint process is voluntary mediation. It cannot force an association to participate or require any outcome."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I get the HOA documents before I buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You should, and most buyers do not. In the state's 2026 report only 32 percent of complainants said they received the governing documents before purchase, while 53 percent got them after. Reading them first is the purpose of the review we run."}}]}</script>
