on{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.55;margin:1.1rem 0 0;padding:0}@media (max-width:699px){.c3-gchart{padding:1rem .95rem .9rem}.c3-gchart .b{width:calc((100% - 6.2rem) * var(--f))}}</style><style>.c3-glance{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--n),minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;max-width:760px}.c3-glance li{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.55rem;margin:0;padding:.5rem .6rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,.55);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;line-height:1.25}.c3-glance .i{flex:0 0 auto;display:flex;width:2rem;height:2rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ivory-2);color:var(--brass-ink);align-items:center;justify-content:center}.c3-glance strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.9rem;font-weight:500}.c3-glance span.t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:1rem}@media (max-width:699px){.c3-glance{gap:.3rem}.c3-glance li{flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-start;text-align:center;gap:.2rem;padding:.4rem .1rem}.c3-glance .i{width:1.6rem;height:1.6rem}.c3-glance strong{font-size:.875rem}.c3-glance span.t{font-size:1rem;line-height:1.2}}</style><ul role="list" class="c3-glance" style="--n:2"><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>4 of 19</strong><span class="t">of one month's Horry County foreclosures were for HOA or condo debts</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>89%</strong><span class="t">of one South Carolina HOA's court bill was lawyers' fees and court costs</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 7, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">How an association forecloses, how often it happens in Horry County, and the steps that stop it before the sale.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. For a house or a condo, a South Carolina homeowners association (HOA) or condo association can put a lien on your home for unpaid dues and ask a court to sell it. A lien is a legal claim for money owed. The lien is on the home itself, so it applies whether or not you have a mortgage.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The bill that starts it can be small. Lawyers' fees and court costs are added to it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you are sued, you usually have 30 days to answer; the summons, the paper served on you, gives the time. Answer, and get the case itself dismissed, because paying the bill does not by itself end the lawsuit. Or sell before the auction.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Behind on HOA dues? Ask us about selling before the auction.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Talk to an agent</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#can-they-foreclose" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Can they foreclose?</a></li><li><a href="#how-often" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How often here</a></li><li><a href="#step-by-step" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Step by step</a></li><li><a href="#how-the-bill-grows" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How the bill grows</a></li><li><a href="#your-mortgage" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Your mortgage</a></li><li><a href="#behind-now" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Behind now</a></li><li><a href="#buying" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Buying</a></li><li><a href="#new-bills" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">New bills</a></li><li><a href="#hoa-liens-and-foreclosure-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy)