are counting on the price to rise</th><td data-label="The better choice">A house</td><td data-label="Why">Houses fell less and grew more</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You want short-term rentals near the ocean</th><td data-label="The better choice">An oceanfront condo</td><td data-label="Why">Oceanfront: 94 in 100 say they allow it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You need an FHA or VA loan</th><td data-label="The better choice">Most likely a house</td><td data-label="Why">Horry County has 9 FHA-approved ones</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You may need to sell within a few years</th><td data-label="The better choice">A house</td><td data-label="Why">Condos sit longer; houses sell sooner</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You do not want to manage a roof, a pool or the building's insurance</th><td data-label="The better choice">A condo</td><td data-label="Why">The HOA takes care of the building</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rest depends on the building: its fee, its rules and whether a lender will finance it. The <a href="#the-building" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">building section</a> below lists what decides one.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="prices-over-time"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices over time">How have Myrtle Beach condo prices done compared with houses?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From its 2006 high to early 2012, the typical metro condo lost about 43 percent of its value. The typical house lost about 30 percent.</p><figure class="c3-gchart c3-cd-fig"><span class="cap" style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .5rem">Typical condo and house value, Myrtle Beach metro</span><ul class="c3-cd-legend"><li><span