 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a 1031 exchange work in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. South Carolina follows the federal 1031 rules, so an investor can defer capital gains tax by exchanging one investment property for another. The Grand Strand is a common replacement market for investors trading out of higher-priced areas."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the 1031 exchange deadlines?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two clocks start the day you close the sale: 45 days to identify replacement properties in writing, and 180 days to close on one. They run together, and missing either deadline makes the deferred gain taxable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need financing for a 1031 exchange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually. To fully defer the tax, the replacement property generally must be equal or greater in value with all proceeds reinvested, which typically requires a loan that closes inside the 180-day window. Because Chapter3 Realty and BrickWood Mortgage work closely together, the purchase and the loan move in step, which helps the replacement close inside the window."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I do a 1031 exchange into multiple properties?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Many investors exchange one appreciated property into several Grand Strand rentals. The identification rules allow up to three properties of any value. You can name more than three only if their combined value stays within 200 percent of the sale, or any number if you close on at least 95 percent of the value identified. A qualified intermediary and a tax advisor should confirm the structure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who holds the money during a 1031 exchange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A qualified intermediary holds the sale proceeds between the two closings. You cannot take possession of the funds, or the exchange is disqualified. This is a tax matter, so professional guidance is essential."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a delayed 1031 exchange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is the standard structure: you sell first, a qualified intermediary holds the proceeds, and you buy the replacement within the 45-day identification window and the 180-day closing window. When people say 1031 exchange, they usually mean a delayed exchange."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does a reverse 1031 exchange work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You buy the replacement first, then sell the old property within 180 days. A company set up by your qualified intermediary holds title to one of the properties in the meantime. Reverse exchanges cost more, fewer lenders finance them, and they need more lead time."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the 45-day or 180-day deadline be extended?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, with one exception: the IRS extends 1031 deadlines only when it issues disaster relief for a presidentially declared disaster. Illness, financing delays, a failed inspection or a seller who backs out do not extend anything. There is a second cutoff too: the exchange must be complete by the due date of your tax return for the year of the sale, including extensions. A late-year seller may need to file a tax extension to keep the full 180 days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I do a 1031 exchange into South Carolina from another state?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The 1031 rules are federal, so you can sell in any state and buy your replacement on the Grand Strand. Plan for South Carolina nonresident withholding at closing; in a proper exchange the closing attorney files an affidavit so you are not over-withheld."}}]}</script>
