k resale</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">344 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.509" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$38,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Resold by a person</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 200 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$15,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The gain between the two deed prices, before repairs, loans, taxes, insurance and both closings. Resales recorded in the 12 months to September 2026. The count is a floor: some sales that may have been a lot that became a new house were left out.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The company figure is the closer guide to an investor's flip. Resales by a person hold most of the losses: about 55 of the 66 homes that sold for less than the earlier deed price. Houses also gained more than condos, a middle of about $56,000 against about $17,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="where"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Where">Where on the Grand Strand do flips happen?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The biggest gains on houses were in Conway, Carolina Forest and Little River. Myrtle Beach had the most quick resales, but most were condos, and the middle one gained about $22,000. No single neighborhood had more than three house resales: the flips are spread across many streets. The deal finder looks at every home for sale for that reason.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" id="c3r-where-homes-were-resold-" viewBox="0 0 360 388" 