 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The three layers of tax on a South Carolina home sale</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Federal:</strong> long-term gains are taxed at 0, 15, or 20 percent depending on income. For 2026, the 0 percent bracket runs to $49,450 of taxable income single and $98,900 married filing jointly, and the 15 percent bracket to $545,500 and $613,700. High earners add a 3.8 percent surtax. If you rented the home out and wrote off wear and tear over the years (called depreciation), the IRS taxes that part back at up to 25 percent when you sell. A primary residence excludes up to $250,000 single or $500,000 married under Section 121 if you owned and lived in it two of the last five years. How the basis and the depreciation were set at purchase is on our <a href="/invest/rental-depreciation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental depreciation page</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">South Carolina:</strong> the state deducts 44 percent of net long-term gains and taxes the rest at its new 2026 top rate of 5.21 percent, an effective rate of roughly 2.92 percent. Anything the federal exclusion wipes out, South Carolina never sees.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">At the closing table, for out-of-state sellers only:</strong> South Carolina collects early. The withholding described below is the piece that surprises absentee owners, and it is manageable with one form.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The withholding</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The SC nonresident withholding: Form I-290, Form I-295, and why the affidavit is everything</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a nonresident sells South Carolina real estate, state law requires the buyer to withhold tax at closing. The closing attorney often does this for the buyer and sends it to the Department of Revenue on Form I-290 by the 15th of the following month. Since a 2024 law change, that rate simply matches South Carolina's top income tax rate: 5.21 percent for a 2026 sale, or 5 percent for a corporation. The old 7 percent figure still on many websites is wrong.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">What the rate applies to is the part sellers can control. Sign the Form I-295 seller's affidavit stating your actual gain, and the withholding is 5.21 percent of the gain. Skip it, and the buyer has to withhold 5.21 percent of the whole sale price (minus your selling costs), capped only by the cash you would walk away with after paying off the mortgage. On a $400,000 sale with a $186,000 gain, that is the difference between roughly $9,700 withheld and roughly $19,600.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Either way it is a prepayment, not the tax. The withholding math ignores the 44 percent deduction, so it almost always takes too much. You file a South Carolina return, report the gain with the deduction, list the amount already withheld, and the state sends back the difference. The withheld amount works like a big W-2. The I-295 also covers the full exemptions: no withholding for a Section 121 primary residence, a 1031 exchange with full deferral, or a certified zero gain, which is exactly the situation of most heirs selling at stepped-up basis. Forms: <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I290.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">I-290</a> and <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I295.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">I-295</a>; statute: <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">S.C. Code 12-8-580</a>.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The withholding</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The SC nonresident withholding: Form I-290, Form I-295, and why the affidavit is everything</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a nonresident sells South Carolina real estate, state law requires the buyer to withhold tax at closing. The closing attorney often does this for the buyer and sends it to the Department of Revenue on Form I-290 by the 15th of the following month. Since a 2024 law change, that rate simply matches South Carolina's top income tax rate: 5.21 percent for a 2026 sale, or 5 percent for a corporation. The old 7 percent figure still on many websites is wrong. A Canadian seller also has a federal withholding, covered on our page on <a href="/invest/canadian-buyers/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">what happens when a Canadian sells a rental here</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">What the rate applies to is the part sellers can control. Sign the Form I-295 seller's affidavit stating your actual gain, and the withholding is 5.21 percent of the gain. Skip it, and the buyer has to withhold 5.21 percent of the whole sale price (minus your selling costs), capped only by the cash you would walk away with after paying off the mortgage. On a $400,000 sale with a $186,000 gain, that is the difference between roughly $9,700 withheld and roughly $19,600.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Either way it is a prepayment, not the tax. The withholding math ignores the 44 percent deduction, so it almost always takes too much. You file a South Carolina return, report the gain with the deduction, list the amount already withheld, and the state sends back the difference. The withheld amount works like a big W-2. The I-295 also covers the full exemptions: no withholding for a Section 121 primary residence, a 1031 exchange with full deferral, or a certified zero gain, which is exactly the situation of most heirs selling at stepped-up basis. Forms: <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I290.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">I-290</a> and <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I295.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">I-295</a>; statute: <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">S.C. Code 12-8-580</a>.</p></div></section>
 
 <section id="cg-calc" style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="padding-top:3rem;padding-bottom:3.2rem"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass-2);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Interactive calculator</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.6rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">South Carolina capital gains and withholding calculator, 2026</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);line-height:1.7;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1.6rem;font-size:.92rem">Assumes a sale in 2026 of property held more than one year. Educational estimate, not tax advice; the 2026 South Carolina forms are not yet published, so state figures are computed from the statute and current forms. Bring your CPA the specifics.</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(200px,1fr));gap:1rem;max-width:980px;margin-bottom:1.4rem"><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-status">Filing status</label><select id="cg-status" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px"><option value="s">Single</option><option value="m">Married filing jointly</option></select></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-use">Property use</label><select id="cg-use" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px"><option value="rental">Rental / investment</option><option value="second">Second home</option><option value="primary">Primary residence (2 of last 5 years)</option><option value="inherited">Inherited (stepped-up basis)</option></select></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-price">Sale price</label><input id="cg-price" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px" inputmode="numeric" value="400,000"></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-cost">Selling costs %</label><input id="cg-cost" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px" inputmode="numeric" value="7"></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-basis">Purchase price / basis</label><input id="cg-basis" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px" inputmode="numeric" value="250,000"></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-imp">Improvements added</label><input id="cg-imp" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-size:.95rem;border-radius:2px" inputmode="numeric" value="0"></div><div><label style="display:block;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(244,239,232,.55);margin-bottom:.35rem;font-weight:600" for="cg-dep">Depreciation taken</label><input id="cg-dep" style="width:100%;background:#fdfbf7;border:1px solid rgba(20,30,40,.25);color:var(--navy);padding:.6rem .7rem;font-