 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you do short-term rentals in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Inside the city, only in the zones where the city allows them, which hold most condos and almost no houses. The city bans rentals under 90 days in every zone for homes, except one zone set aside for visitor stays."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Myrtle Beach a buyer's market?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For condos, yes: there are about 18 months of condos for sale, and prices are falling. For houses, no: there are about 4 months of houses for sale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Carolina Forest in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Carolina Forest has a Myrtle Beach address, but only about 1 in 75 houses and buildings in its ZIP code, 29579, is inside the city."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the property tax on a second home in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"About $1,528 a year for every $100,000 of the county's value inside the city. A home you live in pays about $404."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach are two separate cities with different rental rules. Myrtle Beach allows nightly rentals only in some zones. North Myrtle Beach, for now, asks only for a business license, with no special zoning or permit process beyond it."}}]}</script>
